<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- agent-infra-plist-version: 0.2.0 -->
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!-- worktree-hygiene (#1095 worktree half + #4408 branch half): the recurring
         pass that keeps the tortoise checkout's worktree/branch sprawl bounded.

         WHY A SECOND JOB WHEN com.tortoise.worktree-reaper EXISTS. It does not,
         on this box: nothing is scheduled (measured 2026-10-18 — `launchctl
         list` showed no reaper, `crontab -l` carried only the graph reaper). A
         checkout reaper cannot clear a BRANCH blocker in any case: `git worktree
         remove` never deletes a branch. This job runs both reviewed engines, in
         order.

         ⛔ SHIPS DISARMED — {{WORKTREE_HYGIENE_MODE}} RENDERS the `dry-run` flag.
         This is a DELETION tool on a 6-hourly timer (worktree checkouts, then
         local branch refs), and the recorded rule (#1678, owner ruling
         2026-10-11: "the reaper may run unattended; arming it may not") is that
         such a tool ships dry-run and arming is a SEPARATE, DATED, REVERSIBLE
         step. Three facts make that rule load-bearing here and not ceremonial:
           * `install-launchd.sh` with no arguments installs EVERY template in
             this directory — that is the documented, idempotent, routine
             invocation;
           * `pi-bootstrap/setup.sh` runs exactly that invocation automatically
             on every Darwin box, so a literal `apply` flag in this file would arm
             a fleet from one merge, with no human step and no dry-run
             observation pass;
           * the owner's 2026-10-18 instruction authorised a bounded recurring
             sweep on ONE box. It authorises that box, not the mechanism.
         ARMING, therefore, is per-box and it is a command. ⛔ The exact spelling
         is NOT written out in this comment: XML forbids a doubled hyphen inside
         a comment, so `install-launchd.sh help` (or this file in git, where the
         same text is a shell comment) is the copy-pasteable source. The verb is
         `arm-worktree-hygiene` (optionally followed by its reason argument) and
         it (a) runs the sweep's own preflight mode and REFUSES a box whose
         engine prerequisites do not resolve — an armed job that cannot run exits
         6 every 6h and deletes nothing, silently — and (b) writes a dated record
         (who/when/host/repo/reason) that this placeholder renders from. The
         reversing verb is `disarm-worktree-hygiene`.
         ⛔ NOT AN ENVIRONMENT DEFAULT. The mode is materialised into the
         INSTALLED unit's ProgramArguments, so `install-launchd.sh status` and
         `cat ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.tortoise.worktree-hygiene.plist` both
         answer "is this box deleting anything?" — an env var would arm the job
         from a stray export with nothing on disk to show for it, and a job reads
         no ambient env under launchd anyway.
         ⛔ Do NOT "tidy" this back to a literal apply/dry-run flag: a
         literal cannot be armed (the separate step becomes an untracked
         hand-edit of the installed plist, silently reverted by the next sync)
         and a literal apply flag re-creates the fleet-wide arming this
         placeholder exists to prevent.

         WHY /bin/bash DIRECTLY, NOT THE CHECKOUT'S PYTHON LAUNCHER. The sibling
         reaper templates route through pi-reap-worktrees-launchd.py because
         macOS TCC denies a launchd-spawned bash the read of a script (and a
         git the read of a repo) under ~/Documents. This box's repo is
         /Users/home/eldato/tortoise and this script is under /Users/home — not
         ~/Documents — and the already-loaded com.eldato.nightwatch agent has
         run `/bin/bash <script>` + `git -C /Users/home/eldato/tortoise ...`
         from launchd on this same box for a day. The workaround is therefore
         not needed here; if the estate moves back under ~/Documents, route this
         job through the launcher exactly as the sibling templates do.

         ONE PASS IS BOUNDED. The worktree engine takes
         REAP_WT_BUDGET_SECONDS (1800s; a 300s budget was measured to classify
         22 of 454 rows and defer the rest, i.e. the bound defeated the job's
         purpose) and the reaper bounds every subprocess. Deferred rows are
         PRESERVED, never partially removed. The sweep also probes its own
         PREREQUISITES at the top of every pass (the engine is a FARMED script
         that sources sibling libraries from its own directory): an unresolvable
         prerequisite exits 6 immediately, names the missing path, and writes
         $HOME/.pi/agent/state/worktree-hygiene-blocked, so an inert job is a
         FILE a probe can read rather than an exit code nobody reads.

         WHAT IT WILL NOT REMOVE. Dirty worktrees (tracked or untracked), a
         branch held by any worktree, trunk, an OPEN PR's head, a CLOSED-unmerged
         tip, a tip that advanced beyond its merged PR, or anything a live
         process holds. Remote branches are never touched. -->
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>com.tortoise.worktree-hygiene</string>
    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <string>/bin/bash</string>
        <string>{{HOME}}/.pi/agent/scripts/worktree-hygiene-sweep.sh</string>
        <string>{{WORKTREE_HYGIENE_MODE}}</string>
        <string>--repo</string>
        <string>{{TORTOISE_REPO}}</string>
    </array>
    <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
    <dict>
        <key>PATH</key>
        <string>{{PATH}}</string>
        <key>HOME</key>
        <string>{{HOME}}</string>
        <!-- Both bounds live HERE, not in the operator's shell: launchd gets
             neither the shell env nor its rc files, so an unset variable would
             silently fall back to the 300s/20s defaults measured to make this
             class of job a no-op. -->
        <key>REAP_WT_BUDGET_SECONDS</key>
        <string>1800</string>
        <key>REAP_WT_GH_TIMEOUT</key>
        <string>120</string>
    </dict>
    <key>Nice</key>
    <integer>10</integer>
    <key>ProcessType</key>
    <string>Background</string>
    <key>RunAtLoad</key>
    <false/>
    <key>StartInterval</key>
    <!-- 21600s = 6h. The estate is measured, not guessed: the sibling reaper's
         template records 194 -> 259 -> 294 worktrees across a few DAILY passes,
         i.e. daily was too rare to stop growth while the removal path was
         unusable. 6h bounds a day's accumulation to a quarter-day's growth, and
         a pass costs minutes (332 local branches classified in under 10 min on
         2026-10-18). -->
    <integer>21600</integer>
    <!-- No StandardOutPath/StandardErrorPath: the sweep writes its own capped
         log at $HOME/.pi/agent/state/worktree-hygiene.log, which keeps a
         launchd-owned file from growing without bound. -->
</dict>
</plist>
